      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\nDocument-level info:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03, revision 11\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 4141724\n- Page total: 7\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 28 July 2026\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\nIssuing authority:\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\nApplicants:\n- Badger Meter, Inc., 4545 West Brown Deer Road, Milwaukee, WI 53224, United States of America\n\nManufacturers:\n- Badger Meter Czech Republic s.r.o., Maříkova 2082/26, 62100 Brno, Czech Republic\n\nCertified type:\n- Category: Water meter\n- Type designations: [\"M5000\"]\n- Module designation: null (or maybe not applicable — not mentioned)\n- Description: electromagnetic water meter\n\nRecommendation:\n- R 49-1 (2013) → canonical id \"R49\", edition... Hmm. \"R 49-1 (2013)\". The recommendation is R49 with parts. How to represent? The example says id canonical \"R60\". For R49-1, id could be \"R49-1\" or \"R49\". I think \"R49-1\" is appropriate since the part matters, but the schema says canonical e.g. \"R60\". Let me use \"R49-1\" as the id since that's what's cited, edition 2013 (as int). Hmm, edition is int. 2013 → 2013. Amendment: null. Scheme: A. Accuracy classes: [1, 2].\n\nActually wait — accuracy classes \"1 and 2\" → [1, 2] or [\"1\", \"2\"]? The schema says accuracy_classes[] — I'll use [1, 2] as integers? Hmm, could be strings. OIML R49 accuracy classes are 1, 2, 3. I'll use [\"1\", \"2\"]? Or numbers. I'll go with [1, 2].\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-13200198-01 rev 1, 7 July 2022, 29 pages\n- NMi-3531004-01 rev 1, 4 July 2023, 20 pages\n- NMi-3891220-01, 17 April 2025, 19 pages\n- NMi-3891220-02, 31 July 2025, 18 pages\n- NMi-3954718-01, 3 October 2025, 18 pages\n- NMi-3954718-01R1, 3 October 2025, 19 pages\n- NMi-3948757-01, 23 December 2025, 27 pages\n\nRole: these are type evaluation reports? The certificate says \"tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". The schema role options: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. The certificate references the \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\". These reports are the associated reports. I'll mark them as \"type_evaluation_report\".\n\nDocumentation folder no. T10554-7 — this is a documentation file. Could add to test_reports with role \"documentation_file\". The schema includes documentation_file as a role option. I'll include it: id \"T10554-7\", role \"documentation_file\". No date or pages.\n\nRevision history:\n- Initial, 11 January 2021, \"-\"\n- 1, 17 March 2022, \"Added production location.\"\n- 2, 15 June 2023, \"Extension of line sizes and additional software revisions\"\n- 3, 4 July 2023, \"Correction of small error in Q4 flowrate for DN600\"\n- 4, 17 May 2024, \"Correction of small error in Q4 flowrate for DN200/DN250\"\n- 5, 17 April 2025, \"Addition of the reduced bore version with ratio 400 and software version 2.0.60\"\n- 6, 31 July 2025, \"Addition of horizontal housing, IP68 variant and software version 2.0.61\"\n- 7, 3 October 2025, \"M5000 Facelift - upgrade of electronics housing and external battery version. Addition software version 2.0.62\"\n- 8, 3 October 2025, \"Textual adjustments\"\n- 9, 23 December 2025, \"Evaluation of remote mount version and software update\"\n- 10, 5 March 2026, \"Manufacturer name change and documentation folder update\"\n- 11, 28 July 2026, \"Software update and checksum correction\"\n\nRevision as string: \"Initial\", \"1\" ... \"11\".\n\nFootnotes:\n- The reduced bore footnote: \"* For the reduced bore (RB) version, the size mentioned is the process connection size. On the inscriptions of the water meter the reduced bore meter will be identified as 'DNxxx RB'.\" → marker \"*\" or \"1)\". I'll use \"*\".\n- Table 4 footnote: \"* This display indication range is only available with software revision 2.0.52 or later, other software versions shall not be used for meter size DN600. Additionally, this display indication range is only available with standard cover, IP68 cover shall not be used for meter size DN600.\" → marker \"*\".\n\nTwo footnotes with the same marker \"*\" — hmm. I could differentiate: \"*\" and \"*\". The schema says marker and text. I'll include both entries with marker \"*\". Or maybe give markers \"* (Table 3)\" and \"* (Table 4)\"? That would fabricate. I'll keep both as \"*\". Actually to make them distinguishable, maybe use marker \"* (Table 3)\" — no, that changes the marker. I'll just have two entries with marker \"*\".\n\nNow the characteristics:\n\nType-level (Table 1):\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic flow metering\" (string value)\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" — this is more recommendation-level; but could be type_level characteristic: value \"1 and 2\"? Hmm. It's already in recommendation.accuracy_classes. Maybe include as type_level: accuracy_class: value [\"1\",\"2\"]? The structured value requires value to be number | {min,max} | string. \"1 and 2\" is a string. I could put \"1, 2\". I'll include accuracy_class as string \"1 and 2\". Actually maybe skip since it's in recommendation. But it's a type characteristic too. I'll include it as string.\n\nHmm, let me be careful — the schema says value: <number | {min, max} | string>. So strings allowed for non-numeric. I'll include:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic flow metering\"\n- environmental_class: \"M1 / O\" — with note \"installed outdoors\". Value \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\" or value \"M1 / O\" with a note? I'll keep the full string \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\". Or maybe split: environmental_class \"M1/O\". I'll keep \"M1 / O\" and add footnote? There's no footnote. Keep \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\".\n\nActually let me think about clean canonical labels:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic flow metering\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\"\n- environmental_class: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\" — hmm, string with parens is fine.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C)\" — this is a class T50 with temperature range 0.1-50°C. Hmm. Water temperature class T50 means 0.1°C to 50°C. I could record: water_temperature_class: \"T50\", and water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C. The source says \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\". min 0.1, max 50. I'll do two entries: water_temperature_class: \"T50\" and water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit °C.\n\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa, and also 16 bar. The cell: \"1,6 MPa (16 bar)\". Record maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, unit MPa. Maybe include alternative bar in a second value? I'll do just MPa. Or I could record both: maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa. Keep simple.\n- orientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\" — string.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\" — string.\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" — string. Or boolean true. String is fine.\n- pressure_loss_class: This varies by config: full bore vs reduced bore. Full bore: Δp 10 (0.10 bar); reduced bore: Δp 40 (0.40 bar). This is a config_level attribute with axis \"bore_type\". Conditions: \"full bore\", \"reduced bore\". Values: pressure loss class \"Δp 10\" / \"Δp 40\" and max pressure loss 0.10 bar / 0.40 bar. I'll create config_level entry: attribute \"pressure_loss_class\", axis \"bore_type\", values: [{condition: \"full_bore\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}, {condition: \"reduced_bore\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}]. Hmm, but value structured: value must be number|range|string. \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\" is a string — OK. Alternatively split into pressure_loss_class (\"10\"/\"40\") and max_pressure_loss (0.10/0.40 bar). The Δp class \"Δp 10\" corresponds to 0.10 bar (10 kPa actually — Δp 10 = 0.1 bar = 10 kPa). Let me do:\n  - config_level: attribute pressure_loss_class, axis bore_type, values [{condition \"full bore\", value \"Δp 10\"}, {condition \"reduced bore\", value \"Δp 40\"}]\n  - config_level: attribute maximum_pressure_loss, axis bore_type, unit bar, values [{condition \"full bore\", value 0.1}, {condition \"reduced bore\", value 0.4}]\n- power_supply: \"Internal or external replaceable battery (2.9 – 3.7 V)\". Split: power_supply_type: \"Internal or external replaceable battery\", battery_voltage: {min 2.9, max 3.7} V.\n- software_identification: \"See table 5\" — skip or record as string. I'll record software versions in matrix_tables or as a list. Hmm.\n\nModel family: The family covers many meter sizes DN50–DN600, in class 1 and class 2, plus reduced bore versions (class 2 only). Model IDs: DN50, DN50 RB, DN65, DN65 RB, ..., DN600. \n\nHow to structure? Models: one per variant. There are class-1 variants and class-2 variants, and reduced bore only class 2. Distinct meter sizes: DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600 (14 sizes). Reduced bore variants: DN50 RB, DN65 RB, DN80 RB, DN100 RB (class 2 only).\n\nModel matrix: attributes vary per size and per class. Tables 2 and 3 give per-size flow rates for class 1 and class 2 respectively. Table 4 gives indicating range and verification scale interval per size group.\n\nThe best representation:\n- matrix_tables: three tables (Table 2 class 1 flow rates, Table 3 class 2 flow rates, Table 4 indicating device, Table 5 software versions maybe too).\n- model_family.models: entries for each model variant. How to encode? Model id could be \"DN50\", ..., plus attributes accuracy_class? The models list allows attributes{} per model. I could list models with model_id \"DN50\" attributes {\"accuracy_class\": \"1 and 2\"} etc. But class 1 and class 2 have different flow rates for some sizes (DN150+: class1 vs class2 differ). E.g., DN150 class 1: Q1=3.9375, Q3=630; class 2: Q1=2.52, Q3=630 (wait class2 DN150 Q3=630 too, Q4=787.5 same, but Q1 differs). Actually class 1 DN150: Q1 3.9375, Q2 6.3, Q3 630, Q4 787.5, ratio 160. Class 2 DN150: Q1 2.52, Q2 4.032, Q3 630, Q4 787.5, ratio 250. So flow rates differ per class.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll use model_level characteristics with axis... hmm, model_level values are keyed by \"model\". I could define models as e.g. \"DN150 (class 1)\" and \"DN150 (class 2)\" and \"DN50 (class 2, reduced bore)\". That's 14 + (some sizes in class 2: all 14) + 4 RB = 32 variants. That's a lot but manageable.\n\nAlternatively, use config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\". The schema: config_level is attribute × config-axis matrix with {condition, value}. That could work: attribute qmax (Q3?), axis accuracy_class, values [{condition \"class 1\", value ...}]. But value per condition is single — we need per model too. Combining model × config gets messy.\n\nHmm. Let me simplify: Use model_level for the main flow characteristics keyed by model \"DN50\" ... \"DN600\", using the class 1 values? No — the class 2 values differ.\n\nBetter approach: keep both matrix_tables faithfully (Table 2 and Table 3), and in model_family.models list the variants with attributes including accuracy_class. For model_level characteristics, I can include entries where values are consistent across classes: diameter (Ø in/outlet) per size. And indicating range/verification interval per size (Table 4 applies to both classes presumably).\n\nBut the flow rates differ per class... I could do model_level entries with models named \"DN50/1\" style? Let me think about what's most useful and schema-conforming.\n\nOption: model_family.models entries:\n- {model_id: \"DN50\", attributes: {accuracy_class: [1,2], bore: \"full\"}}\n- {model_id: \"DN50 RB\", attributes: {accuracy_class: [2], bore: \"reduced\"}}\n... etc.\n\nAnd model_level characteristics per attribute:\n- diameter (mm): per DN size — same for class 1 and 2. 14 values.\n- minimum_flow_q1: differs by class → could do two model_level entries? No, one attribute per entry. Values keyed by model... if model ids are DN50 etc., we can't distinguish class.\n\nAlternative: define model ids as compound: \"DN50 class 1\", \"DN50 class 2\", \"DN50 RB class 2\", etc. Then model_level works cleanly: 14 + 14 + 4 = 32 models. Attributes: q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, diameter, indicating_range, verification_scale_interval.\n\nHmm, 32 models × attributes. The values arrays would be long but fine. Actually let me reconsider: Table 2 (class 1) has 14 rows; Table 3 (class 2) has 18 rows (14 full + 4 RB). \n\nFor indicating range (Table 4), groups: DN50 → 99.999 m³ / 0.0001; DN65–DN125 → 999.999 / 0.001; DN150 → 999.999 / 0.01; DN200–DN350 → 9999999 / 0.01; DN400–DN500 → 9999999 / 0.1; DN600 → 99999999* / 0.1 (with footnote).\n\nWait — indicating range values use European thousands separators: \"99.999\" means 99,999 (99 thousand 999)? In Dutch convention, \".\" is thousands separator. \"9.999.999\" = 9,999,999. And verification scale interval \"0,0001\" = 0.0001, \"0,001\" = 0.001, \"0,01\" = 0.01, \"0,1\" = 0.1. So indicating range DN50: 99,999 m³ (5 digits). DN65-125: 999,999 (6 digits). DN200-350: 9,999,999 (7 digits). DN600: 99,999,999 (8 digits). Yes, these are thousands-separated.\n\nSo parse \"99.999\" → 99999, \"999.999\" → 999999, \"9.999.999\" → 9999999, \"99.999.999\" → 99999999.\n\nCareful with DN150: \"999.999\" → 999999, VSI 0.01.\n\nNow flow rates: convert European commas: 0,315 → 0.315; 0,504 → 0.504; 78,75 → 78.75; 312,5 → 312.5; 15,625 → 15.625; 39,375 → 39.375; 50,400 → 50.4; 80,640 → 80.64; 2,52 → 2.52; 4,032 → 4.032; 6,4 → 6.4; 10,24 → 10.24; 25,2 → 25.2; 40,32 → 40.32.\n\nClass 1 table (Table 2):\n- DN50: Ø50, Q1 0.315, Q2 0.504, Q3 63, Q4 78.75, ratio 200\n- DN65: 65, 0.5, 0.8, 100, 125, 200\n- DN80: 80, 0.8, 1.28, 160, 200, 200\n- DN100: 100, 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN125: 125, 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\n- DN150: 150, 3.9375, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160\n- DN200: 200, 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n- DN250: 250, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN300: 300, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN350: 350, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN400: 400, 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\n- DN450: 450, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN500: 500, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN600: 600, 50.4, 80.64, 6300, 7875, 125\n\nClass 2 table (Table 3):\n- DN50: 50, 0.315, 0.504, 63, 78.75, 200\n- DN50 RB: 50, 0.158, 0.25, 63, 78.75, 400\n- DN65: 65, 0.5, 0.8, 100, 125, 200\n- DN65 RB: 65, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n- DN80: 80, 0.8, 1.28, 160, 200, 200\n- DN80 RB: 80, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN100: 100, 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN100 RB: 100, 0.63, 1.00, 250, 312.5, 400\n- DN125: 125, 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\n- DN150: 150, 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n- DN200: 200, 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN250: 250, 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN300: 300, 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN350: 350, 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN400: 400, 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n- DN450: 450, 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN500: 500, 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN600: 600, 40, 64, 10000, 12500, 250\n\nDecision on models: I'll define 32 model variants:\n- For class 1: DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600 with attributes {accuracy_class: 1}\n- For class 2: same 14 sizes + DN50 RB, DN65 RB, DN80 RB, DN100 RB with attributes {accuracy_class: 2}\n\nmodel_id naming: \"DN50 (class 1)\"? Or keep model_id \"DN50\" and duplicate with attributes differentiating? model_id should be unique-ish. I'll use model_id like \"DN50-1\"? Hmm. Better: \"DN50 (accuracy class 1)\". Hmm long. Alternatively \"DN50/C1\". Let me use \"DN50 (class 1)\" style — readable and explicit.\n\nActually, wait. Is it overkill? The certificate treats the family as \"M5000\" with sizes and classes. The model_level characteristics with 32 variants: q1, q2, q3, q4 each with 32 values, ratio with 32, diameter 32 (same per size), indicating_range 32, verification_scale_interval 32. That's 7 attributes × 32 = 224 value entries. Plus matrix_tables duplicating Tables 2-4. That's large but the instructions emphasize matrix detection and populating both. I think it's acceptable. Alternatively, only put flow attributes in matrix_tables and model_level for the distinguishing attributes.\n\nHmm, let me think about the intent: \"matrix_tables: preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" AND \"populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\". The R137 example populates both matrix_tables and model_level. So I'll do both.\n\nTo reduce duplication, for model_level I'll include: diameter, minimum_flow_q1, transitional_flow_q2, permanent_flow_q3, overload_flow_q4, q3_q1_ratio, indicating_range, verification_scale_interval.\n\nUnits: flows m³/h; diameter mm; ratio dimensionless; indicating range m³; verification scale interval m³.\n\nTable 4 groups sizes: DN50; DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125; DN150; DN200..DN350; DN400..DN500; DN600. Applies to both classes (Table 4 is \"indicating device\" generally). DN600 has footnote * on indicating range.\n\nSoftware versions: Table 5 lists 10 + 18 = 28 versions with CRC checksums. This is a table — put in matrix_tables: name \"Approved software versions\", columns [\"software_version\", \"otp_crc\", \"app_crc\"], rows. Let me compile:\n\nPage 5 table:\n1. 9.5.28: OTP bdFc, APP 63b5\n2. 2.0.30: OTP 0571, APP 1644\n3. 2.0.33: OTP 0571, APP 7e5d\n4. 2.0.34: OTP 0571, APP 6D40\n5. 2.0.35: OTP 0571, APP E151\n6. 2.0.37: OTP 0571, APP 019A\n7. 2.0.38: OTP 0571, APP 6902\n8. 2.0.40: OTP 0571, APP 7214\n9. 2.0.42: OTP 0571, APP B459\n10. 2.0.43: OTP 0571, APP 2191\n\nPage 6 table (continuation):\n11. 2.0.44: OTP 0571, APP 5ab6\n12. 2.0.46: OTP 0571, APP E865\n13. 2.0.50: OTP 0571, APP 3cc5\n14. 2.0.51: OTP 0571, APP 6b5c\n15. 2.0.52: OTP 0571, APP 71ca\n16. 2.0.53: OTP 0571, APP 05A2\n17. 2.0.54: OTP CRC 0571, APP 1AD3\n18. 2.0.55: OTP 0571, APP 48BE\n19. 2.0.56: OTP 0571, APP BFF6\n20. 2.0.57: OTP 0571, APP 4C4E\n21. 2.0.58: OTP 0571, APP 846C\n22. 2.0.59: OTP 0571, APP 078E\n23. 2.0.60: OTP 0571, APP 1291\n24. 2.0.61: OTP 0571, APP ECD7\n25. 2.0.62: OTP 0571, APP 027C\n26. 2.0.63: OTP 0571, APP 736E\n27. 2.0.65: OTP 0571, APP CD67\n\nWait that's 10 + 17 = 27. Let me recount page 6: 2.0.44, 2.0.46, 2.0.50, 2.0.51, 2.0.52, 2.0.53, 2.0.54, 2.0.55, 2.0.56, 2.0.57, 2.0.58, 2.0.59, 2.0.60, 2.0.61, 2.0.62, 2.0.63, 2.0.65 = 17 rows. Total 27 software versions.\n\nHmm, \"1291(APP CRC)\" — missing space; value \"1291\".\n\nShould software versions be matrix_tables entries or components? They're approved firmware versions — matrix_tables fits. Or a characteristics list. I'll put into matrix_tables as one combined table (rows 27) with a note that it continues across pages. Or two tables (as in doc, one per page). I'll combine into one table \"Approved software versions\" since page 6 is clearly a continuation (same header). Actually to be faithful, I could do one table. I'll combine.\n\nNow certificate metadata:\n- certificate.number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\"\n- certificate.revision: \"11\"? The schema has certificate fields: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. Revision not listed at cert level but the number includes \"revision 11\". Revision history captures revision \"11\" with date 28 July 2026. I could add \"revision\": \"11\" to certificate — schema doesn't forbid extra fields? Better to stick to schema but adding revision seems natural. The layered schema lists specific keys; I'll include revision_history fully, and maybe add certificate.revision \"11\". Hmm, \"Don't fabricate\" — adding revision is not fabrication, it's present. I'll include it since it's useful. Actually, strictly following schema, certificate keys listed are \"number, scheme (A|B), project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id\". I'll add \"revision\": \"11\" anyway? I'll include it — it's harmless and factual. Let me include.\n\n- scheme: \"A\" (\"issued under scheme A\")\n- project_number: \"4141724\"\n- page_total: 7\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2026-07-28\" (28 July 2026)\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- person_title: null — Certification Board? Not really a title. Omit.\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nApplicants: Badger Meter, Inc. — address_lines: [\"4545 West Brown Deer Road\", \"Milwaukee, WI 53224\", \"United States of America\"]\n\nManufacturers: Badger Meter Czech Republic s.r.o. — [\"Maříkova 2082/26\", \"62100 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"]\n\nCertified type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"M5000\"]\n- module_designation: null — omit? The category options given: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", ... The certified type is a water meter (complete measuring instrument). Not a module. I'll omit module_designation or set null. I'll omit... Actually setting null is fine per schema. I'll set to null.\n\nHmm — the M5000 might consist of sensor + calculator, but the cert doesn't break it into modules. Omit.\n\n- description: \"Electromagnetic water meter\" — from \"Identification of the certified type: An electromagnetic water meter\".\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"M5000\"? The type M5000 is the family. \"include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant\" — yes it does. family_name: \"M5000\".\n- models: the 32 variants as decided. Hmm, is 32 too granular? Let me reconsider: maybe models keyed by size only (14) with attribute accuracy_class: [1, 2], and use matrix_tables for per-class flow rates. Then model_level characteristics only for attributes that don't vary by class (diameter, indicating range, verification interval). The flow rates per class live in matrix_tables. That's cleaner and still faithful.\n\nBut rule 4 says extract varying attributes into model_level... The varying dimension here is size × class. I think a hybrid: model_level entries for q1/q2/q3/q4 with 32 entries (models distinguished by class) is the most complete. But it duplicates matrix_tables. The R137 example duplicates (matrix_tables AND model_level). So duplication is expected.\n\nLet me decide: models = 32 variants with model_id like \"DN50 class 1\", \"DN50 class 2\", \"DN50 RB class 2\". Actually maybe simpler: \"DN50-1\"? I'll use \"DN50 (class 1)\", \"DN50 (class 2)\", \"DN50 RB (class 2)\". Hmm RB implies class 2. Fine.\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n1. diameter: values per 32 models? Diameter same per size across classes — 32 entries redundant. Could do 14 entries with model \"DN50\"... but model ids now include class. Mixing key styles is bad. Let me do all model_level values keyed by the full model ids (32). Diameter 32 entries where e.g. DN50 (class 1) → 50, DN50 (class 2) → 50, DN50 RB (class 2) → 50. Fine, it's mechanical.\n\nActually, hmm, this is getting really long. Total values: diameter 32, q1 32, q2 32, q3 32, q4 32, ratio 32, indicating_range 32, verification_interval 32 = 256 value objects. Plus matrix_tables rows (14+18+6+27=65 rows). Large JSON but OK.\n\nAlternatively reduce: model_level only for attributes that vary across models, which all of these do vary (across sizes). OK.\n\nLet me reconsider indicating range per Table 4 mapping to sizes:\n- DN50: 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125: 999999, 0.001\n- DN150: 999999, 0.01\n- DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350: 9999999, 0.01\n- DN400, DN450, DN500: 9999999, 0.1\n- DN600: 99999999 (footnote *), 0.1\n\nNow, reduced bore variants — do they share indicating ranges? Table 4 says \"Meter size\" generically; RB meters are identified as DNxxx RB; presumably same display. I'll 