l\":\"DN250\",\"attributes\":{\"accuracy_class\":2,\"bore\":\"full\"}},\n {\"model_id\":\"DN300 (class 2)\",\"label\":\"DN300\",\"attributes\":{\"accuracy_class\":2,\"bore\":\"full\"}},\n {\"model_id\":\"DN350 (class 2)\",\"label\":\"DN350\",\"attributes\":{\"accuracy_class\":2,\"bore\":\"full\"}},\n {\"model_id\":\"DN400 (class 2)\",\"label\":\"DN400\",\"attributes\":{\"accuracy_class\":2,\"bore\":\"full\"}},\n {\"model_id\":\"DN450 (class 2)\",\"label\":\"DN450\",\"attributes\":{\"accuracy_class\":2,\"bore\":\"full\"}},\n {\"model_id\":\"DN500 (class 2)\",\"label\":\"DN500\",\"attributes\":{\"accuracy_class\":2,\"bore\":\"full\"}},\n {\"model_id\":\"DN600 (class 2)\",\"label\":\"DN600\",\"attributes\":{\"accuracy_class\":2,\"bore\":\"full\"}}\n]\n\nmodel_level entries — 8 attributes:\n\n1. inlet_outlet_diameter, unit mm — 32 values (or 14?). All 32 keyed by model id.\nActually to save space maybe diameter as 14 entries keyed by size without class... but model keys must match model ids. I'll do all 32. Hmm, that's a lot of typing but let me do it. Actually, wait — do I really need diameter at model level given it's in matrix_tables? The model_level duplicates key info. I'll include q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, inlet_outlet_diameter, indicating_range, verification_scale_interval. Yes 8.\n\nLet me write them.\n\ndiameter values (order: C1 14 then C2 18):\nC1: DN50 50, DN65 65, DN80 80, DN100 100, DN125 125, DN150 150, DN200 200, DN250 250, DN300 300, DN350 350, DN400 400, DN450 450, DN500 500, DN600 600\nC2: DN50 50, DN50 RB 50, DN65 65, DN65 RB 65, DN80 80, DN80 RB 80, DN100 100, DN100 RB 100, DN125 125, DN150 150, DN200 200, DN250 250, DN300 300, DN350 350, DN400 400, DN450 450, DN500 500, DN600 600\n\nq1 values:\nC1: 0.315, 0.5, 0.8, 1, 1.6, 3.9375, 6.25, 10, 15.625, 15.625, 25, 39.375, 39.375, 50.4\nC2: 0.315, 0.158, 0.5, 0.25, 0.8, 0.4, 1, 0.63, 1.6, 2.52, 6.4, 6.4, 10, 10, 16, 25.2, 25.2, 40\n\nq2 values:\nC1: 0.504, 0.8, 1.28, 1.6, 2.56, 6.3, 10, 16, 25, 25, 40, 63, 63, 80.64\nC2: 0.504, 0.25, 0.8, 0.4, 1.28, 0.64, 1.6, 1, 2.56, 4.032, 10.24, 10.24, 16, 16, 25.6, 40.32, 40.32, 64\n\nq3 values:\nC1: 63, 100, 160, 250, 400, 630, 1000, 1600, 2500, 2500, 4000, 6300, 6300, 6300\nC2: 63, 63, 100, 100, 160, 160, 250, 250, 400, 630, 1600, 1600, 2500, 2500, 4000, 6300, 6300, 10000\n\nq4 values:\nC1: 78.75, 125, 200, 312.5, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875, 7875, 7875\nC2: 78.75, 78.75, 125, 125, 200, 200, 312.5, 312.5, 500, 787.5, 2000, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875, 7875, 12500\n\nq3_q1_ratio:\nC1: 200, 200, 200, 250, 250, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 125\nC2: 200, 400, 200, 400, 200, 400, 250, 400, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250\n\nindicating_range (m³):\nDN50 C1 99999; DN65 999999; DN80 999999; DN100 999999; DN125 999999; DN150 999999; DN200 9999999; DN250 9999999; DN300 9999999; DN350 9999999; DN400 9999999; DN450 9999999; DN500 9999999; DN600 99999999*\nC2: DN50 99999; DN50 RB 99999; DN65 999999; DN65 RB 999999; DN80 999999; DN80 RB 999999; DN100 999999; DN100 RB 999999; DN125 999999; DN150 999999; DN200 9999999; DN250 9999999; DN300 9999999; DN350 9999999; DN400 9999999; DN450 9999999; DN500 9999999; DN600 99999999*\n\nverification_scale_interval (m³):\nDN50: 0.0001 (all three DN50 variants)\nDN65–DN125 (incl RB): 0.001\nDN150: 0.01\nDN200–DN350: 0.01\nDN400–DN500: 0.1\nDN600: 0.1\n\nAttribute labels: use canonical: \"minimum_flow_q1\", \"transitional_flow_q2\", \"permanent_flow_q3\", \"overload_flow_q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"diameter_inlet_outlet\", \"indicating_range\", \"verification_scale_interval\". For matrix table column names, similar: \"meter_size\", \"diameter\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\". Hmm consistency: use same labels in both? In the R137 example, matrix columns were qmax/qt/qmin and model_level attributes qmax etc. So use short names consistently: q1/q2/q3/q4? But canonical labels like \"minimum_flow_q1\" are more descriptive. I'll use \"minimum_flow_q1\" etc. in both matrix columns and model_level attributes for consistency.\n\nmatrix_tables columns for Table 2/3: [\"meter_size\", \"inlet_outlet_diameter\", \"minimum_flow_q1\", \"transitional_flow_q2\", \"permanent_flow_q3\", \"overload_flow_q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\"], column_units [null, \"mm\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null].\n\nRow keys must match columns.\n\nTable 4 columns: [\"meter_sizes\", \"indicating_range\", \"verification_scale_interval\"], units [null, \"m³\", \"m³\"].\n\nTable 5 columns: [\"software_version\", \"otp_crc_checksum\", \"app_crc_checksum\"], units [null, null, null]. description notes combined from pages 5–6.\n\nNow type_level characteristics final list:\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic flow metering\"}\n- environmental_class: {value: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\"} — hmm, maybe value \"M1 / O\" and note? I'll keep full string. Actually cleaner: value \"M1 / O\", with a second characteristic? No. Keep \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\".\n\nHmm wait — R49-1 environmental classes: the notch letter for outdoor... \"M1 / O\" — M1 is mechanical, O is climatic? Actually in R49, climatic and mechanical environmental classes combined. I'll keep the string as-is.\n\n- electromagnetic_environment: {value: \"E2\"}\n- ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"} — the source says \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\". min 0.1. OK.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"}\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\"}\n- power_supply: {value: \"Internal or external replaceable battery\"} — and battery_supply_voltage: {value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.7}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n\nconfig_level:\n[\n {attribute: \"pressure_loss_class\", axis: \"bore_type\", values: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: \"Δp 10\"}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: \"Δp 40\"}]},\n {attribute: \"maximum_pressure_loss\", axis: \"bore_type\", unit_symbol: \"bar\", values: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: 0.1}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: 0.4}]}\n]\n\nWait, config values structured: \"{condition, value, unit_symbol}\" per rule 5. I'll include unit_symbol in each value for the pressure loss one.\n\nAlso maybe accuracy_class at type level: {value: \"1 and 2\"}? It's in recommendation already. Skip.\n\nCertificate object:\n{\n \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\",\n \"revision\": \"11\",\n \"scheme\": \"A\",\n \"project_number\": \"4141724\",\n \"page_total\": 7,\n \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n \"date_issued\": \"2026-07-28\",\n \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n\nIssuing authority:\n{\n \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n \"address_lines\": [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n \"person_responsible\": \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\",\n \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n\ntest_reports:\n[\n {\"id\": \"NMi-13200198-01\", \"date\": \"2022-07-07\", \"pages\": 29, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"NMi-3531004-01\", \"date\": \"2023-07-04\", \"pages\": 20, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"NMi-3891220-01\", \"date\": \"2025-04-17\", \"pages\": 19, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"NMi-3891220-02\", \"date\": \"2025-07-31\", \"pages\": 18, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"NMi-3954718-01\", \"date\": \"2025-10-03\", \"pages\": 18, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"NMi-3954718-01R1\", \"date\": \"2025-10-03\", \"pages\": 19, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"NMi-3948757-01\", \"date\": \"2025-12-23\", \"pages\": 27, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"T10554-7\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n]\n\nrevision_history:\n[\n {\"revision\": \"Initial\", \"date\": \"2021-01-11\", \"changes\": null},\n {\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2022-03-17\", \"changes\": \"Added production location.\"},\n {\"revision\": \"2\", \"date\": \"2023-06-15\", \"changes\": \"Extension of line sizes and additional software revisions\"},\n {\"revision\": \"3\", \"date\": \"2023-07-04\", \"changes\": \"Correction of small error in Q4 flowrate for DN600\"},\n {\"revision\": \"4\", \"date\": \"2024-05-17\", \"changes\": \"Correction of small error in Q4 flowrate for DN200/DN250\"},\n {\"revision\": \"5\", \"date\": \"2025-04-17\", \"changes\": \"Addition of the reduced bore version with ratio 400 and software version 2.0.60\"},\n {\"revision\": \"6\", \"date\": \"2025-07-31\", \"changes\": \"Addition of horizontal housing, IP68 variant and software version 2.0.61\"},\n {\"revision\": \"7\", \"date\": \"2025-10-03\", \"changes\": \"M5000 Facelift - upgrade of electronics housing and external battery version. Addition software version 2.0.62\"},\n {\"revision\": \"8\", \"date\": \"2025-10-03\", \"changes\": \"Textual adjustments\"},\n {\"revision\": \"9\", \"date\": \"2025-12-23\", \"changes\": \"Evaluation of remote mount version and software update\"},\n {\"revision\": \"10\", \"date\": \"2026-03-05\", \"changes\": \"Manufacturer name change and documentation folder update\"},\n {\"revision\": \"11\", \"date\": \"2026-07-28\", \"changes\": \"Software update and checksum correction\"}\n]\n\nFor \"Initial\" changes: \"-\" → null.\n\nfootnotes:\n[\n {\"marker\": \"*\", \"text\": \"For the reduced bore (RB) version, the size mentioned is the process connection size. On the inscriptions of the water meter the reduced bore meter will be identified as \\\"DNxxx RB\\\".\"},\n {\"marker\": \"*\", \"text\": \"This display indication range is only available with software revision 2.0.52 or later; other software versions shall not be used for meter size DN600. Additionally, this display indication range is only available with standard cover; IP68 cover shall not be used for meter size DN600.\"}\n]\n\nKeep text verbatim-ish. The original: \"This display indication range is only available with software revision 2.0.52 or later, other software versions shall not be used for meter size DN600. Additionally, this display indication range is only available with standard cover, IP68 cover shall not be used for meter size DN600.\" Keep commas as source.\n\nrecommendation:\n{\"id\": \"R49-1\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]}\n\nHmm accuracy_classes: maybe strings [\"1\",\"2\"]. The R49 classes are denoted 1, 2, 3. I'll use numbers [1, 2]. Actually accuracy class values could be \"1\" or \"2\" as strings for safety... The example in schema: accuracy_classes[] with no type. Numbers fine.\n\ncertified_type:\n{\n \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n \"type_designations\": [\"M5000\"],\n \"description\": \"Electromagnetic water meter (electromagnetic flow metering), accuracy class 1 and 2.\"\n}\n\nKeep description simple: \"An electromagnetic water meter\" — verbatim. Good.\n\nNow write the full model_level. This is long; let me carefully construct.\n\nModel order (consistent everywhere):\n1. DN50 (class 1)\n2. DN65 (class 1)\n3. DN80 (class 1)\n4. DN100 (class 1)\n5. DN125 (class 1)\n6. DN150 (class 1)\n7. DN200 (class 1)\n8. DN250 (class 1)\n9. DN300 (class 1)\n10. DN350 (class 1)\n11. DN400 (class 1)\n12. DN450 (class 1)\n13. DN500 (class 1)\n14. DN600 (class 1)\n15. DN50 (class 2)\n16. DN50 RB (class 2)\n17. DN65 (class 2)\n18. DN65 RB (class 2)\n19. DN80 (class 2)\n20. DN80 RB (class 2)\n21. DN100 (class 2)\n22. DN100 RB (class 2)\n23. DN125 (class 2)\n24. DN150 (class 2)\n25. DN200 (class 2)\n26. DN250 (class 2)\n27. DN300 (class 2)\n28. DN350 (class 2)\n29. DN400 (class 2)\n30. DN450 (class 2)\n31. DN500 (class 2)\n32. DN600 (class 2)\n\nOK. Also, indicating_range attribute values — footnote marker \"*\" for DN600 entries (both).\n\nNow, one consideration: unit_symbol at model_level attribute level (not per value). In R137 example: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [...]}. Yes, unit at attribute level.\n\nFor indicating_range unit \"m³\" and verification_scale_interval \"m³\".\n\nLet me now also double-check the temperature: \"-25 °C / +55 °C\" → min -25, max 55. Yes.\n\nMAP: \"1,6 MPa (16 bar)\" → 1.6 MPa. I'll also add a second type-level entry? I decided MPa only. Hmm, actually maybe include the bar variant as attribute \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" is 1.6 MPa — done. Skip bar.\n\nNow assemble final JSON. I need to be careful with JSON validity — no trailing commas, proper escaping of quotes in footnote text (\"DNxxx RB\" — need \\\" escaping).\n\nAlso \"Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\" — keep as \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\".\n\nThe issuing authority name on page 1 header: \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\". I'll use name \"NMi Certin B.V.\" and oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\".\n\nDescription of matrix table 2: include the \"flow rates freely chosen\" note. Add to both Table 2 and Table 3 descriptions.\n\nAlso note in Table 3 description: reduced bore identified as \"DNxxx RB\" on inscriptions (footnote).\n\nmatrix_tables entries:\n\n1.\n{\n \"name\": \"General characteristics of the family of instruments with accuracy class 1\",\n \"columns\": [\"meter_size\",\"inlet_outlet_diameter\",\"minimum_flow_q1\",\"transitional_flow_q2\",\"permanent_flow_q3\",\"overload_flow_q4\",\"q3_q1_ratio\"],\n \"column_units\": [null,\"mm\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",null],\n \"rows\": [14 rows],\n \"description\": \"Flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3 and Q4 can be freely chosen as long as: Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 are selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013(E); values for Q1 and Q2 are minimum values with ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6; values for Q3 and Q4 are maximum values with ratio Q4/Q3 = 1.25; the ratio Q3/Q1 is at least 40.\",\n \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\n2. Same but class 2, 18 rows, footnotes: [\"*\"] with description mentioning reduced bore.\n\n3.\n{\n \"name\": \"General characteristics of the indicating device\",\n \"columns\": [\"meter_sizes\",\"indicating_range\",\"verification_scale_interval\"],\n \"column_units\": [null,\"m³\",\"m³\"],\n \"rows\": [\n  {\"meter_sizes\":\"DN50\",\"indicating_range\":99999,\"verification_scale_interval\":0.0001},\n  {\"meter_sizes\":\"DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125\",\"indicating_range\":999999,\"verification_scale_interval\":0.001},\n  {\"meter_sizes\":\"DN150\",\"indicating_range\":999999,\"verification_scale_interval\":0.01},\n  {\"meter_sizes\":\"DN200 till DN350\",\"indicating_range\":9999999,\"verification_scale_interval\":0.01},\n  {\"meter_sizes\":\"DN400 till DN500\",\"indicating_range\":9999999,\"verification_scale_interval\":0.1},\n  {\"meter_sizes\":\"DN600\",\"indicating_range\":99999999,\"verification_scale_interval\":0.1}\n ],\n \"description\": null or note,\n \"footnotes\": [\"*\"]\n}\n\nHmm \"DN200 till DN350\" — Dutch \"till\" = \"to\". Keep as source? \"DN200 till DN350\" reads odd but it's source. I'll use \"DN200–DN350\"? Keep source \"DN200 till DN350\". Fine.\n\n4.\n{\n \"name\": \"Approved software versions\",\n \"columns\": [\"software_version\",\"otp_crc_checksum\",\"app_crc_checksum\"],\n \"column_units\": [null,null,null],\n \"rows\": [27 rows],\n \"description\": \"Approved firmware/software versions with OTP and application CRC checksums (table spans certificate pages 5–6).\",\n \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\nNow, let me double check numbers with European decimal commas in flow tables:\n- 0,315 → 0.315 ✓\n- 0,504 → 0.504 ✓\n- 78,75 → 78.75 ✓\n- 312,5 → 312.5 ✓\n- 3,9375 → 3.9375 ✓\n- 15,625 → 15.625 ✓\n- 39,375 → 39.375 ✓\n- 50,400 → 50.400 → 50.4 ✓\n- 80,640 → 80.64 ✓\n- 2,52 → 2.52 ✓\n- 4,032 → 4.032 ✓\n- 6,4 → 6.4 ✓\n- 10,24 → 10.24 ✓\n- 25,2 → 25.2 ✓\n- 40,32 → 40.32 ✓\n- 0,0001 → 0.0001 ✓\n- 0,001 → 0.001 ✓\n- 0,01 → 0.01 ✓\n- 0,1 → 0.1 ✓\n- 1,6 MPa → 1.6 ✓\n- 2,9 – 3,7 V → 2.9–3.7 ✓\n- 0,10 bar → 0.1 ✓; 0,40 → 0.4 ✓\n\nIndicating ranges with dots as thousand separators: 99.999 → 99999; 999.999 → 999999; 9.999.999 → 9999999; 99.999.999 → 99999999. ✓ (Dutch formatting.)\n\nWait — is that right? Could \"99.999\" mean 99.999 (i.e., ~99.999 m³ with decimal)? For a DN50 water meter with verification interval 0.0001 m³ (0.1 L), indicating range 99.999 m³ with 4 decimals makes sense: 99999.9999... Actually typical R49 display: 99999.9999 m³ for VSI 0.0001 m³. Yes indicating range is 99999 (five integer digits). And for VSI 0.001 → 999999 (6 digits × 0.001 = 999.999 max displayed). Yes standard. So thousands separators confirmed.\n\nNow, JSON size — it'll be big. That's fine.\n\nLet me also decide whether to include \"accuracy_class\" in type_level. I'll include: {\"attribute\": ... } type_level: accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"}? Hmm, might be redundant but harmless. I'll skip to keep type_level clean — it's in recommendation. Actually many extractors include it. I'll skip.\n\nAlso environmental_class — value \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\". Hmm alternatively \"M1 / O\". I'll use \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\" verbatim.\n\nLet me also double check person_title — none given. Omit.\n\nCertificate: also there's \"PRODUCTS RvA C 681\" — accreditation stuff, skip.\n\nNow write model_level arrays carefully.\n\nattribute \"inlet_outlet_diameter\" unit \"mm\":\nvalues:\n{\"model\":\"DN50 (class 1)\",\"value\":50},\n{\"model\":\"DN65 (class 1)\",\"value\":65},\n{\"model\":\"DN80 (class 1)\",\"value\":80},\n{\"model\":\"DN100 (class 1)\",\"value\":100},\n{\"model\":\"DN125 (class 1)\",\"value\":125},\n{\"model\":\"DN150 (class 1)\",\"value\":150},\n{\"model\":\"DN200 (class 1)\",\"value\":200},\n{\"model\":\"DN250 (class 1)\",\"value\":250},\n{\"model\":\"DN300 (class 1)\",\"value\":300},\n{\"model\":\"DN350 (class 1)\",\"value\":350},\n{\"model\":\"DN400 (class 1)\",\"value\":400},\n{\"model\":\"DN450 (class 1)\",\"value\":450},\n{\"model\":\"DN500 (class 1)\",\"value\":500},\n{\"model\":\"DN600 (class 1)\",\"value\":600},\n{\"model\":\"DN50 (class 2)\",\"value\":50},\n{\"model\":\"DN50 RB (class 2)\",\"value\":50},\n{\"model\":\"DN65 (class 2)\",\"value\":65},\n{\"model\":\"DN65 RB (class 2)\",\"value\":65},\n{\"model\":\"DN80 (class 2)\",\"value\":80},\n{\"model\":\"DN80 RB (class 2)\",\"value\":80},\n{\"model\":\"DN100 (class 2)\",\"value\":100},\n{\"model\":\"DN100 RB (class 2)\",\"value\":100},\n{\"model\":\"DN125 (class 2)\",\"value\":125},\n{\"model\":\"DN150 (class 2)\",\"value\":150},\n{\"model\":\"DN200 (class 2)\",\"value\":200},\n{\"model\":\"DN250 (class 2)\",\"value\":250},\n{\"model\":\"DN300 (class 2)\",\"value\":300},\n{\"model\":\"DN350 (class 2)\",\"value\":350},\n{\"model\":\"DN400 (class 2)\",\"value\":400},\n{\"model\":\"DN450 (class 2)\",\"value\":450},\n{\"model\":\"DN500 (class 2)\",\"value\":500},\n{\"model\":\"DN600 (class 2)\",\"value\":600}\n\nminimum_flow_q1 unit \"m³/h\":\nC1: DN50 0.315, DN65 0.5, DN80 0.8, DN100 1, DN125 1.6, DN150 3.9375, DN200 6.25, DN250 10, DN300 15.625, DN350 15.625, DN400 25, DN450 39.375, DN500 39.375, DN600 50.4\nC2: DN50 0.315, DN50 RB 0.158, DN65 0.5, DN65 RB 0.25, DN80 0.8, DN80 RB 0.4, DN100 1, DN100 RB 0.63, DN125 1.6, DN150 2.52, DN200 6.4, DN250 6.4, DN300 10, DN350 10, DN400 16, DN450 25.2, DN500 25.2, DN600 40\n\ntransitional_flow_q2:\nC1: 0.504, 0.8, 1.28, 1.6, 2.56, 6.3, 10, 16, 25, 25, 40, 63, 63, 80.64\nC2: 0.504, 0.25, 0.8, 0.4, 1.28, 0.64, 1.6, 1, 2.56, 4.032, 10.24, 10.24, 16, 16, 25.6, 40.32, 40.32, 64\n\npermanent_flow_q3:\nC1: 63, 100, 160, 250, 400, 630, 1000, 1600, 2500, 2500, 4000, 6300, 6300, 6300\nC2: 63, 63, 100, 100, 160, 160, 250, 250, 400, 630, 1600, 1600, 2500, 2500, 4000, 6300, 6300, 10000\n\noverload_flow_q4:\nC1: 78.75, 125, 200, 312.5, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875, 7875, 7875\nC2: 78.75, 78.75, 125, 125, 200, 200, 312.5, 312.5, 500, 787.5, 2000, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875, 7875, 12500\n\nq3_q1_ratio (dimensionless):\nC1: 200, 200, 200, 250, 250, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 125\nC2: 200, 400, 200, 400, 200, 400, 250, 400, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250\n\nindicating_range unit \"m³\":\nC1: DN50 99999, DN65 999999, DN80 999999, DN100 999999, DN125 999999, DN150 999999, DN200 9999999, DN250 9999999, DN300 9999999, DN350 9999999, DN400 9999999, DN450 9999999, DN500 9999999, DN600 99999999 (footnote [\"*\"])\nC2: DN50 99999, DN50 RB 99999, DN65 999999, DN65 RB 999999, DN80 999999, DN80 RB 999999, DN100 999999, DN100 RB 999999, DN125 999999, DN150 999999, DN200 9999999, DN250 9999999, DN300 9999999, DN350 9999999, DN400 9999999, DN450 9999999, DN500 9999999, DN600 99999999 (footnote [\"*\"])\n\nverification_scale_interval unit \"m³\":\nDN50 variants: 0.0001\nDN65–DN125 (all incl RB): 0.001\nDN150: 0.01\nDN200–DN350: 0.01\nDN400–DN500: 0.1\nDN600: 0.1\n\nOrder:\nC1: DN50 0.0001, DN65 0.001, DN80 0.001, DN100 0.001, DN125 0.001, DN150 0.01, DN200 0.01, DN250 0.01, DN300 0.01, DN350 0.01, DN400 0.1, DN450 0.1, DN500 0.1, DN600 0.1\nC2: DN50 0.0001, DN50 RB 0.0001, DN65 0.001, DN65 RB 0.001, DN80 0.001, DN80 RB 0.001, DN100 0.001, DN100 RB 0.001, DN125 0.001, DN150 0.01, DN200 0.01, DN250 0.01, DN300 0.01, DN350 0.01, DN400 0.1, DN450 0.1, DN500 0.1, DN600 0.1\n\nOK. Time to write the JSON. I must be very careful with syntax. Let me draft it fully.\n\nOne more thought: should \"accuracy_class\" also be in model attributes? I included in models[].attributes. Good.\n\nAlso family_name: \"M5000\".\n\nFinal JSON structure:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {...},\n  \"issuing_authority\": {...},\n  \"applicants\": [{...}],\n  \"manufacturers\": [{...}],\n  \"certified_type\": {...},\n  \"model_family\": {...},\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {...},\n    \"model_level\": [...],\n    \"config_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...],\n  \"recommendation\": {...},\n  \"test_reports\": [...],\n  \"revision_history\": [...],\n  \"footnotes\": [...]\n}\n\nFor type_level each value must be structured object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. E.g.:\n\n\"measuring_principle\": {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic flow metering\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}\n\nShould I include unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers keys when absent? The rule 2 shape shows them. To be safe include \"unit_symbol\" only where relevant and footnote_markers only when nonempty? The shape is strict: \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\". I'll include all keys everywhere (footnote_markers [] when none, unit_id null). That makes it verbose but conformant. Hmm — for model_level values, the example in the prompt shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} without unit keys (unit at attribute level). So model_level values don't need unit keys. For config_level: \"{condition, value, unit_symbol}\" — include unit_symbol per value.\n\nFor type_level, include full shape: value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers. I'll include footnote_markers only when present? Rule 7 says record markers when referenced. The shape includes footnote_markers — I'll include with [] default. Actually to reduce verbosity maybe include only when non-empty... The shape shows footnote_markers in the object. I'll include footnote_markers: [] everywhere in type_level for strictness. Hmm, that's fine.\n\nActually, let me include \"footnote_markers\" only when there are markers, to keep it readable... but \"STRICT\" says shape. I'll include it always as []. Compromise: include. It's fine.\n\nFor model_level attribute-level values, no unit keys (matching example). OK.\n\nLet me write out the whole thing now.\n\nType level:\n\n\"type_level\": {\n  \"measuring_principle\": {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic flow metering\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n  \"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\", ...},\n  \"electromagnetic_environment\": {\"value\": \"E2\", ...},\n  \"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n  \"water_temperature_class\": {\"value\": \"T50\", ...},\n  \"water_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", ...},\n  \"maximum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\", ...},\n  \"orientation\": {\"value\": \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\", ...},\n  \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\": {\"value\": \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\", ...},\n  \"reverse_flow\": {\"value\": \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\", ...},\n  \"power_supply\": {\"value\": \"Internal or external replaceable battery\", ...},\n  \"battery_supply_voltage\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 2.9, \"max\": 3.7}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", ...}\n}\n\nConfig level:\n\n\"config_level\": [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"pressure_loss_class\",\n    \"axis\": \"bore_type\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore\", \"value\": \"Δp 10\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore\", \"value\": \"Δp 40\", \"unit_symbol\": null}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"maximum_pressure_loss\",\n    \"axis\": \"bore_type\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore\", \"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore\", \"value\": 0.4, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\nNow write everything. Also matrix table 2 description — I'll add the conditions text.\n\nLet me now carefully produce the final JSON. I'll be methodical to avoid syntax errors. Given length, I'll write compactly (one value per line for long arrays is fine).\n\nAlso check: \"Ratio Q3/Q1\" attribute label \"q3_q1_ratio\" — good.\n\nDouble-check DN100 class 2 RB: Q1 0.63, Q2 1.00 → 1.0. ratio 400. Yes.\n\nDN600 class 1: Q3 6300, Q4 7875 (revision 3 corrected DN600 Q4). Table 2 DN600 row: 600, 50,400 → 50.4, 80,640 → 80.64, 6300, 7875, 125. Yes.\n\nDN450/500 class1: Q1 39.375, Q2 63, Q3 6300, Q4 7875, ratio 160 ✓.\n\nWrite JSON now.\n\nAlso I should double check the software table rows: 9.5.28 OTP \"bdFc\" APP \"63b5\". Yes.\n\nOne nuance: \"1291(APP CRC)\" → app_crc \"1291\".\n\nOK, final answer. Let me write it.",
